{ The Anglo-Lithuanian was recently recommended by @ByronJimenez as a good variation to add, so I will, and I hope you enjoy this latecomer to this study (2 years in the future). My writing will be more mature than it was then (being two teenage years older). }
1. c4 Nc6 { The point of 1...Nc6 (the defining move of the Anglo-Lithuanian Variation) is to lock up the center after 2. d4 e5 3. d5 Nce7, followed by ...Ng6 and ...d6, with possible kingside attacking chances. If you want to avoid all this, you can follow the common narrative of this study and play 2. Nc3. } 2. Nc3 e5 { Which will quickly transpose into the Reversed Sicilian variation. } *

[Event "English Opening!: PS: English Opening: Anglo-Lithuanian Variation (Part 2)"]
