[Orientation "white"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ [%cal Gc2c4] }
1. c4 Nc6 { We begin where we left off from in Chapter 13. If you want to challenge Black's idea, the main move is 2. d4. } 2. d4 e5 { Attack Black's knight in the center. } 3. d5 Nce7 { From this position, White has two main options: 4. Nc3 and 4. e4. They are not much different in terms of objective value, but 4. Nc3 is more flexible, because the e-pawn may be best on e3 or e4, so let's play Nc3. } 4. Nc3 { Black may in this position play 4...Ng6 or 4...d6, but both of these moves can be met in a similar way. } { [%cal Rd7d6,Re7g6] } (4. e4 { Not wrong, but less flexible. Play Nc3 to continue. }) 4... Ng6 { Now, we may follow the moves that have been laid out in previous chapters: fianchetto your light-squared bishop. } 5. g3 Bc5 { It doesn't really matter what Black plays here, but 5...Bc5 is most common.
Continue your fianchetto. } 6. Bg2 d6 { White's space advantage is somewhat balanced by Black's attacking chances. } *

[Event "English Opening!: PS: English Opening: Anglo-Lithuanian Variation (Part 3)"]
[Date "2025.01.18"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "A40"]
[Opening "Mikenas Defense: Lithuanian Variation"]
