[ChapterName "PS: English Opening: Anglo-Lithuanian Variation (Part 3)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/qkiEGvJu/KtLpWJke"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/ChessBeginner210"]
[UTCDate "2025.01.18"]
[UTCTime "15:09:06"]
[Orientation "white"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ [%cal Gc2c4] }
1. c4 Nc6 { [%cal Gd2d4] } 2. d4 e5 3. d5 Nce7 { [%cal Gb1c3] } 4. Nc3 Ng6 { I was examining this position with Stockfish when I found a neat computer idea: 5. Qa4! } 5. Qa4! { This chapter may be more advanced, so you can click off if it ever gets confusing.
The point of 5. Qa4 is that it pins the d-pawn, making it difficult for Black to develop. The light-squared grip White has already built up makes it very difficult to break the pin, so the best way to do that would be to castle. But White has another idea to make this work. } 5... Nf6 { White's very interesting idea in this position to keep Black off-balance is to gain some tempi on the knights: can you see how? } 6. h4! { 5...Be7 6. g3 would lead to the same idea, supported by the g3 pawn. } 6... h5 { To prevent h5. 6...Be7?! is inaccurate due to 7. h5 Nf8 (7...Nf4? loses elegantly to 8. e3! N4xh5 9. g4!, winning the knight due to the trap and overload).
But Black's last move left a positional weakness: can you exploit it? } 7. Bg5 { And White has a solid and enduring positional advantage. } *

