[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/ChessBeginner210"]
[UTCDate "2021.11.24"]
[UTCTime "22:38:40"]
[Orientation "white"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ The Grunfeld Defense is a common problem for d4 players. (Well, not at my level, where nobody knows how to play hypermodern openings, but at ~2000 elo.) So I'm going to show you how to play c4 to beat the Grunfeld. You remember our starting move, right? }
1. c4 Nf6 { Grunfeld players look to use this setup highlighted in red. Play Nc3. } { [%cal Rg8f6,Rg7g6,Rd7d5,Gb1c3] } 2. Nc3 g6 { Here, if you like playing against the Grunfeld (like me), then you play d4 and transpose into it. But if you want to avoid it entirely, then you play e4. } { [%cal Rd2d4,Ge2e4] } 3. e4 { This will soon transpose into a King's Indian Defense, a much different opening than the Grunfeld. } *

